<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  MS1.3 least-privilege hardened-runtime entitlement manifest (the single source MS9 signs with).

  DERIVED, not "whatever it needs". Each key is justified by a concrete feature; a key is added
  ONLY after the Mac build proves the feature cannot run without it. Nothing speculative.

  Pinned at:       thoughts/.../plans/2026-10-14-14-30-macos-launch-tdd-plan.md (MS1.3, rev 6, ed13e5e)
  Verified on Mac: NOT YET. The Intel Mac is offline; the "derived" set below is the minimum known
                   from the architecture and MUST be reconciled against a real ad-hoc codesign
                   entitlements dump on the Mac before MS1 goes green (the MS1.3 assertion).

  Candidate keys held OUT until the Mac proves them (do NOT add on a guess):
    * com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit and allow-unsigned-executable-memory.
      WKWebView runs JS in Apple's own signed com.apple.WebKit.WebContent XPC service, so the
      host app likely does NOT need JIT entitlements. Confirm on the Mac (load a JS-heavy page
      under hardened runtime); add ONLY if WebContent fails to attach without it.
    * com.apple.security.inherit.
      We confine the helper, CLI and hooks with Seatbelt (sandbox-exec, MS6), not App Sandbox
      inheritance, so this is expected to stay OUT.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!-- Outbound network: the CLI/helper reach the egress proxy on the backend's loopback port,
         and the browser itself fetches over the network (MS6 egress capability, rev 6). -->
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
